<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Tomara dynasty of Gwalior</h1><p class="st-meta">1394 CE – 1523 CE · capital Gwalior</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Rajput dynasty that ruled from the fortress of Gwalior, whose king Man Singh built the Man Mandir palace and patronised dhrupad music.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bir-singh-deo-tomar">Bir Singh Deo</a>, a Tomara chief serving the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>, seized the fort of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gwalior-state">Gwalior</a> around 1394, during the confusion that followed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firuz-shah-tughluq">Firuz Shah Tughluq's</a> death and preceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timur">Timur's</a> invasion. His descendants held Gwalior as an independent kingdom, at times paying tribute to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sayyid-dynasty">Sayyid</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lodi-dynasty">Lodi</a> sultans of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi">Delhi</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dungarendra-singh">Dungarendra Singh</a> had the colossal Jain rock-cut statues carved into the cliffs of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gwalior-state">Gwalior</a> fort. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/man-singh-tomar">Man Singh Tomar</a>, the most famous ruler, built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/man-mandir-palace">Man Mandir</a> palace with its blue tile work and sponsored music; the dhrupad style and the singer tradition later associated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tansen">Tansen</a> developed at his court. His queen Mrignayani is remembered in legend.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibrahim-lodi">Ibrahim Lodi</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a> besieged <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gwalior-state">Gwalior</a> and forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikramaditya-tomar">Vikramaditya Tomar</a> to surrender in 1523. Vikramaditya later died fighting for Ibrahim at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-battle-of-panipat">First Battle of Panipat</a> in 1526. Gwalior passed to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mughal-empire">Mughal Empire</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="kingdom, 1394 CE – 1523 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 72 V40 L36 54 L50 28 L64 54 L78 40 V72 Z"/><path d="M22 80 H78"/><circle cx="50" cy="24" r="3"/><circle cx="22" cy="36" r="2.5"/><circle cx="78" cy="36" r="2.5"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">kingdom</p><p class="st-emblem-region">South Asia</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:87.44%;width:2.57%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1394 CE – 1523 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Gwalior</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Bir Singh Deo, Dungarendra Singh, Man Singh Tomar</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism, Jainism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Hindi (Braj)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Tomaras of Gwalior, Tomar Rajputs of Gwalior</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1458&amp;focus=tomara-dynasty-of-gwalior" aria-label="Tomara dynasty of Gwalior on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6744 1215 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Tomara dynasty of Gwalior lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7064 1471L7018 1385"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7018 1385h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7064 1471h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7064 1471h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Gwalior. <a href="/atlas?year=1458&amp;focus=tomara-dynasty-of-gwalior">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
